To limit the number of items per row in a Flutter list or grid view, you can use the `Row` or `GridView` widget with a custom `ItemCountListener`. Here's an example using the `GridView` widget:
```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        appBar: AppBar(title: Text('Limit Items per Row')),
        body: GridViewExample(),
      ),
    );
  }
}

class GridViewExample extends StatefulWidget {
  @override
  _GridViewExampleState createState() => _GridViewExampleState();
}

class _GridViewExampleState extends State<GridViewExample> {
  int _itemCount = 0;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Container(
      child: GridView.count(
        crossAxisCount: 2,
        children: List.generate(100, (index) {
          return Container(
            color: Colors.amber,
            width: 60,
            height: 60,
            child: Text('Item $index'),
          );
        }),
        itemCount: _itemCount,
        physics: NeverScrollableScrollPhysics(),
        shrinkWrap: true,
        onItemCountChanged: (int oldItemCount, int newItemCount) {
          setState(() {
            _itemCount = newItemCount;
          });
        },
      ),
    );
  }
}
```
In this example, we use the `GridView` widget with a `GridView.count` and a custom list of items. The `itemCount` property is set to `_itemCount`, which is a state variable. The `onItemCountChanged` callback updates the `_itemCount` variable when the item count changes. The `crossAxisCount` property is set to `2` to create a 2x2 grid, and the `physics` property is set to `NeverScrollableScrollPhysics()` to prevent scrolling. The `shrinkWrap` property is set to `true` to fill the available space with the grid view.

Adjust the values of `crossAxisCount`, `physics`, `shrinkWrap`, and the list of items to meet your specific requirements.

